From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lars Wikman <lars@underjord.io>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 22:46:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: virtio: return -ENXIO when no message went through

When the device fails the first message of a transfer (no device
acknowledged the address), virtio_i2c_xfer() returns 0, the number of
messages done. i2c-dev passes that on, and I2C_RDWR users that only check
for a negative return (Circuits.I2C, for one) take it for success, so a
scan finds a device at every address. Return -ENXIO, which adapters use
for an address that was not acknowledged.
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-virtio.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-virtio.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-virtio.c
index af13819..8dffdef 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-virtio.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-virtio.c
@@ -159,6 +159,14 @@ static int virtio_i2c_xfer(struct i2c_adapter *adap, struct i2c_msg *msgs,
 
 	count = virtio_i2c_complete_reqs(vq, reqs, msgs, count);
 
+	/*
+	 * Not even the first message went through: nothing answered at that
+	 * address. Say so as other adapters do (see fault-codes.rst) instead
+	 * of returning 0, which I2C_RDWR users take for success.
+	 */
+	if (!count)
+		count = -ENXIO;
+
 err_free:
 	kfree(reqs);
 	return count;
-- 
